REGENT OF LABUHANBATU SELATAN,

Considering : a. that violence against women and children in Labuhanbatu Selatan Regency continues to increase, causing community members to feel unsafe in carrying out their lives, thus requiring integrated protection efforts;
b. that in order to optimize the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence in Labuhanbatu Selatan Regency, it is necessary to provide direction, foundation, and legal certainty to all parties involved in the implementation of protection for women and children;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children from Acts of Violence.

In View of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
4. Law Number 20 of 1999 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention Number 138 Concerning Minimum Age For Admission To Employment (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 56, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3835);
5. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
6. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 237, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5946);
7. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
8. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635) as amended by Law Number 31 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5602);
9. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
10. Law Number 22 of 2008 concerning Establishment of Labuhanbatu Selatan Regency in North Sumatra Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4868);
11. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
12. Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
13. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
14. Presidential Regulation Number 25 of 2021 concerning Child-Friendly Regency/City Policy (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2021 Number 96);
15. Regional Regulation of Labuhanbatu Selatan Regency Number 9 of 2016 concerning Establishment of Regional Apparatus of Labuhanbatu Selatan Regency (Regional Gazette of Labuhanbatu Selatan Regency of 2016 Number 9 Series D Number 09, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Labuhanbatu Selatan Regency Number 09) as amended by Regional Regulation of Labuhanbatu Selatan Regency Number 5 of 2021 concerning Amendment to Regional Regulation of Labuhanbatu Selatan Regency Number 9 of 2016 concerning Establishment of Regional Apparatus of Labuhanbatu Selatan Regency (Regional Gazette of Labuhanbatu Selatan Regency of 2021 Number 5 Series D Number 05, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Labuhanbatu Selatan Regency Number 05).

With Mutual Agreement

Article 5
The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:
a. forms of Violence;
b. rights of Victims;
c. obligations and responsibilities;
d. implementation of protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence;
e. coordination and cooperation;
f. participation of the Community and business sector;
g. guidance and supervision;
h. reporting; and
i. sources of funding.

Article 26
(1) Forms of Services provided to Women and Children Victims of Violence are as follows:
a. Complaint services;
b. Health services;
c. legal aid;
d. return;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medico-legal;
f. Identification services; and
g. Psychological services.
(2) Forms of Services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with minimum Service standards established by the government and implemented by regional apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social;
b. health;
c. Women's empowerment and Child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual.
(3) In carrying out duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), regional governments cooperate with government agencies, provincial governments, other district/city governments, the Community, Families, and parents.
(4) Further provisions regarding procedures for Services and the handling of Women and Children Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3) shall be regulated by a Regent's Regulation.

Article 33
To implement the protection of Women and Children, the Regional Government shall conduct Gender Mainstreaming which includes:
a. providing guidance for regional apparatus in formulating Gender integration strategies carried out through planning, preparation, implementation, budgeting, monitoring and evaluation of development policies, programs and activities.
b. developing a Gender-based information system;
c. facilitating institutional strengthening and development of Gender Mainstreaming mechanisms in government institutions, women's study centers, research and development institutions, as well as non-governmental institutions;
d. implementing development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and social affairs that take into account Gender aspects and are Child-friendly;
e. conducting protection of Women especially protection for Victims of Violence, Women workers, Women Victims of human trafficking, elderly Women, and Women with disabilities, and areas affected by disasters; and
f. facilitating the strengthening and development of networks of community institutions or organizations and the business sector for the implementation of Gender mainstreaming.

Article 16
(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the Community as referred to in Article 13 letter b, shall be implemented in the form of Community participation.
(2) The form of Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
   a. establishing family partners at the Kelurahan/Village level;
   b. establishing women and children protection units within community organizations;
   c. conducting independent socialization of the rights of Women and Children;
   d. providing first aid to Victims; and
   e. reporting to the competent authorities if Violence against Victims occurs in their environment.
(3) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, private sector, and mass media.
(4) The form of Community participation as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be implemented responsibly in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 19
(1) P2TP2A as referred to in Article 18 consists of elements from the Community, higher education institutions, professional organizations, and community organizations/social organizations concerned with social issues.
(2) The formation of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by a Regent’s decree.
(3) The provision of Services to Victims shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Village/Sub-district P2TP2A, the District P2TP2A, and the Regency P2TP2A.
(4) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) has the duty to:
a. receive complaints/reports of Violence against Women and/or Children, consultation, and counseling;
b. receive and forward them to other Service units in a tiered manner;
c. provide legal assistance and accompaniment;
d. provide health services;
e. provide Social Rehabilitation;
f. provide legal services;
g. provide repatriation and social reintegration; and
h. provide protection to Victims, reporters, and witnesses.
(5) P2TP2A may provide temporary services and protection in the form of a Safe House.
(6) In the event that P2TP2A does not yet have a Safe House as referred to in paragraph (5), Victims of Violence shall be referred to a P2TP2A that has a Safe House.
(7) P2TP2A consists of several Divisions comprising:
a. Complaints Service Division;
b. Health Service Division;
c. Legal Aid Consultation Division; and
d. Rehabilitation, Repatriation, and Social Reintegration Division.

Article 40
(1) The Regent coordinates the protection of Women and Children against acts of Violence with:
a. the government;
b. regency/city regional governments;
c. vertical agencies;
d. educational institutions; and
e. community institutions.
(2) Coordination as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. religious affairs;
b. health affairs;
c. education affairs;
d. social affairs;
e. legal affairs;
f. labor affairs; and
g. other affairs.
(3) Coordination as referred to in paragraph (2) is implemented by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their authority.

Article 21
Regional Government conducts prevention of acts of Violence against Women and Children, through:
a. socialization activities of laws and regulations to the Community related to the protection of the rights of Women and Children;
b. conducting empowerment and awareness to Families, parents and the Community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling;
c. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
d. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social Services;
e. opening employment opportunities for Women;
f. building participation and concern of the Community to implement prevention and protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence;
g. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
h. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various community non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for Women and Children;
i. opening complaint posts for protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence; and
j. training of P2TP2A members related to the implementation of their duties in conducting prevention of Violence against Women and Children.

Article 41
(1) In certain circumstances, the Regional Government may cooperate with: a. other regional governments;
b. the police;
c. advocates;
d. health workers;
e. social workers;
f. assistant/companion volunteers;
g. spiritual advisers;
h. other law enforcers;
i. the National Commission on Violence against Women;
j. the Indonesian Child Protection Commission or Child Protection Institutions; and/or
k. certain parties desired for the benefit of the Victim,
to assist the Victim in the process at court hearings.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of: a. socialization;
b. provision of legal aid;
c. health services;
d. assistance to the Victim;
e. counseling;
f. social rehabilitation;
g. spiritual guidance; and/or
h. return and social reintegration.

Article 23
Forms of P2TP2A Services include:
a. Medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of Victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. Psychosocial services are services provided in order to restore the traumatic condition of Victims, including the provision of Safe Houses to protect Victims from various threats and intimidation against Victims and to provide social support so that Victims have self-confidence, strength and independence in resolving their problems;
c. Medico-legal services are a form of medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
d. Legal services to assist Victims in undergoing legal processes; and
e. Economic independence services in the form of skills training and providing economic access so that Victims can be independent.

Article 22
(1) Prevention of the occurrence of Violence against Women and Children as referred to in Article 21, shall be implemented by Regional apparatus whose duties and functions are in the field of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. Women’s Empowerment and Child Protection;
f. mental and spiritual; and
g. public tranquility and order.
(2) Prevention of Violence by Regional apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional action plan.
(3) Further provisions regarding the mechanism for implementing prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by a Regent Regulation.

